{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790264656,"nonce":"KrVkTbHxlAFKoBUwIJBpj7POZG4=","title":"The runner: one player, fresh each stop, and an executive function Dorian writes","message":"Decided with Dorian on 2026-09-24, after comparing against zorrobyte/rimagent. **Keep the mod and replace the driver.** Today one long Claude Code session types `rwa` commands for hours through filters it writes itself. The 09-02 audit measured the cost of that:\n- 2,546 of 2,988 `rwa` calls went through the agent's own `python3 -c` filter, so it read the filter's output rather than the reply (COCKPIT.md, How it stays honest).\n- Every standing rule decayed across the run. Per quarter, find-rect went from 22 to 2 and saves from 33 to 8 (themes.md T11).\n- Handovers between sessions dropped exactly the knowledge that had been expensive to get (T15).\n\n**What it is**\nA program that owns the run, and the only thing that calls `advance`. After each advance it reads the screen (975973e) and evaluates the executive function. If anything needs the player, it starts one fresh Claude call for that stop.\n\n**Rulings (Dorian, 2026-09-24)**\n- **One player.** No role split.\n- **The executive function is deterministic and lives outside the game.** It holds the anticipatory reminders a player gives themselves before the game's own late alerts fire: \"you don't wait til you're starving to find food\". Dorian molds it by watching rounds. The agent never edits it.\n- **Fresh context at every stop:** the rules, the screen, and the player's notebook. Continuity lives in the notebook. Asked whether that wastes tokens: no. A long session re-reads its whole growing history on every call, while a fresh stop re-reads only a cached fixed prefix plus that stop's own calls.\n- **No self-editing.** No agent-written rules, no improvement pass, no patching the harness.\n\n**Requirements**\n1. **The rules file.** The executive function is a file Dorian edits. Each rule is a trigger over screen or digest paths, written in the same predicate grammar as `advance until:{condition}` (fc287ba), plus a reminder. A rule may also stop the clock, which the runner passes as the next advance's `until`. The first version is seeded from `checklists/` and the run contract's standing rules, and every seeded rule cites the line it came from.\n2. **The player** is `claude -p` on Dorian's subscription. Not `--bare`, because bare mode only takes an API key. Its context holds only what the runner gives it: the rules, the notebook, the screen and the reminders. No repo development instructions leak in.\n3. **Tools, no shell.** The game's verbs reach the player as tools whose replies land whole. The player has no shell, so it cannot filter a reply.\n4. **Ending a stop.** The player rewrites its notebook and states a wake condition. The runner owns the next advance.\n5. **One record per stop**, at `RUNS/\u003crun\u003e/stops/\u003cn\u003e.json`. It holds the screen the player saw, the rules that fired, every call and reply, the notebook diff, and the usage `--output-format json` reports. The cockpit viewer can replay it.\n6. **The game never sits paused with nobody acting.** The clock stands still only while the player is thinking. Between stops the runner keeps advancing. This is the standing never-pause rule.\n\n**Acceptance**\n- A bench run of at least one in-game day in which every stop is a fresh `claude -p` call and every verb call went through the tools, with zero through a shell.\n- One stop record per stop, each of which replays in the cockpit viewer.\n- A rule Dorian adds between two stops fires on the next stop, with no restart.\n- Tokens per stop and per in-game day are reported from the records, whatever they are.\n- `d2e1229` (the play-loop doc) is marked superseded, and says what by.\n\n**Depends on:** d7d9c57 and 50df87a (new game and load, in progress) to start and resume a run, and 975973e's screen (first half built).\n**Cross-refs:** b4adee2 (the cockpit round), ffef0d7 (chores run below the runner), 70ee75e (the client).","files":null}]}